
GNU Autotools build
-------------------
To build and install using the tarball, extract the files from
the tarball, change directory to the top level within the
extracted tree, and then:
  # ./autogen.sh
  # ./configure
  # make
  # make install

The './bootstrap' script can be used in place of './autogen.sh' .
The last command will most likely require root privileges and will
place the binaries in the /usr/local/bin directory. Since the utilities
need root (superuser) permissions some distributions prefer to place the
binaries in the /usr/sbin directory. To do that and place the man pages
in their normal (Linux) location the ./configure line could be expanded
to:
  # ./configure --prefix=/usr --bindir=/usr/sbin

To clean up after a build use the following command:
  # make distclean


The build depends on libtool since a shared library is produced. A static
library is also produced. By default the binaries (i.e. the utilities
in this package) will depend on that shared library. The binaries can
be linked instead with the static library with this invocation:
  # ./configure --disable-shared

cmake build
-----------
This build is experimental, feedback encouraged. Firstly this cmake build
is NOT intended to replace Autotools, the intention is that both will be
supported and not interfere with one another.

The primary configuration file in cmake is called CMakeLists.txt and there
is one in the top level directory (of the source package) and others in
the lib, src, include and doc directories. There is another "magic"
configuration file call config.h.in.cmake that is explained in the
top-level CMakeLists.txt file.

To start the build process with cmake, in the simplest case call:
    # cmake .

Options can be requested like this
    # cmake . -DENABLE_DEBUG=ON

[The available options can be viewed with 'cmake -LH .' . for this
 package it can be seen that ENABLE_DEBUG is default OFF .]

The second step is to build the utilities, in the simplest case call:
    # cmake --build .

The shared library is in the lib directory and the utility executables
are in the src directory.

As an alternative or after 'cmake --build .' DEB and RPM packages can
be built for Linux with:
    # cpack .

There are three packages each for DEB and RPM. One package has the
utilities, man pages and some general information. A second package
contains the shared library need for runtime. The third package
is the "dev" (or "devel") version of the library that includes the
project's public include files. That would be useful for another
project that may want to use libsmputils1 .

To clean up the build (artifact) files:
    # cd <package_top_level_directory>
    # make clean
    # ./cmake_del_artifacts.sh 

Cmake support for other platforms is coming, starting with FreeBSD.


